{"attack":true,"case":"The target formulates the relation as 'integrates' (a compatibility/integration relation) yet defines its refutation condition as 'Variance Floor is purely individual cognitive effect rather than a collective panel effect' — that refutes the dependence of the effect on the panel, not the integration of the two floors.\nThe problem asks for a substantive relation naming its kind and stating what refutes it. The target picks integration but its 'refuted if' negates the source attribution of one endpoint, not the integration itself; integration is refuted if the mechanisms conflict or cannot be combined, not merely if one factor has an individual-level cause. So the stated refutation does not correspond to the named relation kind; at most it establishes a conditional dependence (panel-level iff non-individual), not integration.\ncites: 322ec812da42f394f49faa8bf167e5eb98772226cd5afd34d8f9e15a02c5bbd4","counterexample":["Let A = 'Variance Floor depends on panel oversight' and B = 'Algorithmic Floor = checklist-driven execution'. Suppose the Variance Floor is in fact a purely individual cognitive effect, so A is false. If the checklist still works for individuals and the individual variance-floor effect remains compatible with the checklist, B and the floor-level compatibility persist; integration of A and B is not thereby refuted, only A's dependence on the panel is. Thus the target's own 'refuted if' fails to negate its claimed 'integration' relation."]}